#. Paragraph text in the Masters Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/masters/masters.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use KUMAR Masters</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>The catalogue and the network. Change things here and the rest of the system follows - warranty periods, model specs, who a dealer reports to.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>Pump Category</b> sets the default warranty months for a whole family.</li><li><b>Pump Model</b> holds the real specs from the brochure and can override the category's warranty. Each model points at one stock Item.</li><li><b>Dealer Tree</b> is a hierarchy: Branch - Distributor - Dealer - Sub-Dealer. A dealer login sees its own records and everything below it, nothing sideways.</li><li><b>Settings</b> holds every assumption - SLA hours, warranty basis, QC enforcement, QR base URL. Nothing is hardcoded.</li><li><b>Historical Serial Import</b> is for go-live: download the CSV template, fill in the pumps you built and sold before this system existed, check the file, then import. Rows with a sale date come in as submitted registrations with the warranty already running.</li><li><b>Stock vs Registration Reconciliation</b> is the list of loose ends afterwards - above all the pumps that have LEFT the building with no registration behind them, so their warranty never started.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Give a dealer a login by setting <b>Portal User</b> on the Dealer record. That single field is what row-level access is derived from.</li><li>Always run the check step before importing. A registration is submitted, so a wrong warranty date can only be cancelled and amended afterwards, never quietly deleted.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Management Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/management/management.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use Management</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>Six screens that read the same month from six angles. Nothing here is typed in - every number is computed from the documents the plant and the dealers are already raising, so if a figure looks wrong the answer is always in the document behind it. Click any row to open the document it came from.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>Management Dashboard</b> is the daily one: revenue, spend, margin, output, quality, complaints and the wage bill on one screen. It answers <i>how are we doing</i>; the other five answer <i>why</i>.</li><li><b>Sales Analytics</b> - revenue by day, best models, top dealers and customers, and the receivable ageing. The order-to-invoice funnel shows where a sale is stuck: ordered but not delivered, or delivered but not billed.</li><li><b>Purchase Analytics</b> - spend by supplier and material, what has been ordered and not received, and what has been received and not billed.</li><li><b>Daily Production</b> - units built per day against units tested and passed, split by shift, with the foundry and winding lines underneath.</li><li><b>Dealer Network</b> - every dealer ranked, with a fault rate per dealer and the districts the pumps actually landed in. Silent dealers are the ones to ring.</li><li><b>People &amp; Payroll</b> - headcount by department, attendance for the period, and what the last payroll run cost.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Every screen defaults to the last 30 days. Change the period at the top - the whole screen, tiles and tables included, moves with it.</li><li>Money tiles are shortened to lakhs and crores so they read at a glance; hover for the exact rupee figure.</li><li>A dealer login never sees this workspace. Dealers get <b>My Business</b>, which shows only their own network.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Production Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/production/production.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use Stock &amp; Manufacturing</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>The ERPNext side of the plant, in the order a pump is actually built: buy the components, melt and wind, raise a Work Order against a BOM, consume the batches, produce a serialised pump, test it, then dispatch it. Nothing here is a KUMAR invention - it is standard ERPNext, wired so the serial and batch numbers flow.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>Item</b> - a finished pump has <i>Has Serial No</i> with the series <code>KP-&lt;MODEL&gt;-.YY..MM.-.#####</code>; casings, stators and rotors have <i>Has Batch No</i>. The <b>Traceability Group</b> field on the item is what tells the genealogy hook which slot a consumed part fills.</li><li><b>Purchase Order &rarr; Purchase Receipt</b> for bought-out parts (bearings, seals, capacitors). Supplier lots come in as batches.</li><li><b>BOM</b> then <b>Work Order</b> for a production run. Print the <b>Route Card</b> from the Work Order and let it travel with the job.</li><li><b>Stock Entry (Manufacture)</b> consumes the casing and stator batches and produces the pump. Serial numbers are generated here, and the heat and winding numbers are stamped onto each one automatically.</li><li><b>Pump Test Certificate</b> per unit, then <b>Delivery Note</b> / <b>Sales Invoice</b>. A unit that has not passed test cannot be delivered.</li><li><b>Stock Ledger</b>, <b>Stock Balance</b> and <b>Batch-wise Balance History</b> answer where anything is; <b>Serial No</b> answers what one unit is.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Do not type serials into the old text field - v16 keeps them in a Serial and Batch Bundle. Use the scan button or the bundle selector.</li><li>A Manufacture entry that consumes no traceable batch will warn you. Fix it before submitting; that gap cannot be filled in later.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Traceability Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/traceability/traceability.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use Traceability & Quality</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>The factory half. A pump's identity is built here: which melt its casing came from, which winding lot its stator came from, and whether it passed test. Get this right and any field failure can be traced back to a root batch.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>Heat Record</b> - log the melt, enter spectrometer readings, and only then set status to Approved for Pouring. Out-of-spec elements block approval unless a Quality Engineer records an override reason.</li><li><b>Winding Batch</b> - log the stator lot with its IR and HiPot readings.</li><li>Assembly consumes those batches in a Manufacture Stock Entry. The heat and winding numbers are stamped onto every serial produced automatically - no typing.</li><li><b>Test Certificate</b> - one per unit. On submit it sets the serial's QC status, and a unit that has not passed cannot be put on a Delivery Note.</li><li><b>Trace a Serial</b> for one pump's ancestry; <b>Batch Defect Analysis</b> for the reverse - every pump built from a suspect batch.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Approving a heat with an override is a deliberate, recorded act. Use it rarely.</li><li>If a Manufacture entry warns that no traceable batches were consumed, fix it before submitting - that gap is permanent.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Warranty Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/warranty/warranty.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use Warranty & Claims</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>Where a dealer's claim is checked and settled, and where warranty exposure is watched. Each claim carries the pump's heat number and winding batch, which is what makes it possible to spot a bad batch instead of arguing about one pump.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>A dealer submits a claim; it lands in <b>Pending Claims</b>.</li><li>Service Manager reviews it into Under Investigation. Quality Engineer approves or rejects on the evidence - photos, technician report, root cause.</li><li>Approved claims go to Accounts to <b>Settle</b>, optionally against a credit note.</li><li>Check <b>Batch Defect Analysis</b> whenever two claims share a heat or winding batch.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Rejecting without a root cause loses the only information the factory would get.</li><li><b>Warranty Expiring (30d)</b> is a sales list, not a problem list - it is the AMC and replacement opportunity.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Dealer Desk Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/dealer_desk/dealer_desk.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use the Dealer Desk</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>This is the dealer's whole job in one screen: record the sale of a pump so its warranty starts, and raise a complaint when a customer calls. Registering a pump is what generates the warranty certificate in KUMAR Pumps' name.</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>Register Pump</b> - scan or type the serial from the nameplate. Model, HP and manufacturing date fill themselves. Add the customer's name and mobile, the sale date and the installation address, then Submit.</li><li>The warranty expiry is calculated for you from the model's warranty period. Print the <b>Warranty Certificate</b> from the print icon - it carries the QR code.</li><li><b>New Service Request</b> - enter the serial and the warranty banner tells you immediately whether the visit is free or chargeable.</li><li><b>My Registrations</b> and <b>My Claims</b> show only your own network's records.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>A serial can only be registered once. If the customer changes, amend the existing registration rather than creating a second one.</li><li>The mobile number must be a real 10-digit number - it is what the warranty is traced by.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

#. Paragraph text in the Service Desk Workspace
#: kumar_service/kumar_service/workspace/service_desk/service_desk.json
msgid "<div style='border:1px solid var(--border-color);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--fg-color)'><p style='margin:0 0 6px'><b>How to use the Service Desk</b></p><p style='margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--text-muted)'>Everything after a customer complains: triage it, put a technician on it, and close it inside the SLA the company promises (24 hours to respond).</p><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Do this, in order</b></p><ol style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li>Open the complaint from <b>Open Requests</b>. The pump's full history is already on the form - check <b>Repeat Failure</b> before assuming it is a fresh fault.</li><li>Set <b>Technician</b> and <b>Service Centre</b>, then set status to Assigned. The response clock stops when you fill <b>First Response</b>.</li><li>The technician records what happened in a <b>Service Visit</b>: findings, parts used, labour. Warranty jobs default to non-chargeable.</li><li>Close the request with a <b>Root Cause</b>. That field is what feeds the Pareto chart and tells the factory what to fix.</li></ol><p style='margin:10px 0 4px'><b>Watch out for</b></p><ul style='margin:0 0 4px 18px'><li><b>SLA Breaches</b> is the list to clear first thing every morning.</li><li>If the root cause is a Manufacturing Defect, raise a Warranty Claim so the batch gets analysed - otherwise the plant never learns.</li></ul></div>"
msgstr ""

